WB.L("barcodes-qr-codes", "technology", "beginner", "Barcodes and QR codes: reading the stripes", "Lines drawn in beach sand, a pack of chewing gum in Ohio and a Japanese board game all helped create the codes you scan every day.",
  [
    ["Lines drawn in sand", "The barcode began as an idea for speeding up grocery checkouts in the late 1940s.",
      "In 1948, the head of an American food chain reportedly asked a dean at the Drexel Institute of Technology in Philadelphia whether checkouts could be automated. A graduate student, Bernard Silver, overheard and told his friend Norman Joseph Woodland. Woodland later recalled that the breakthrough came on a Miami beach. Thinking of the Morse code he had learned as a Boy Scout, he pushed his fingers into the sand and dragged them towards himself, turning dots and dashes into thin and thick lines. The pair filed a patent in 1949, granted in 1952, which included a circular 'bullseye' version that could be read from any direction. But nothing could yet read it cheaply. Practical barcodes had to wait more than two decades for lasers and microchips."],
    ["A pack of gum makes history", "The first product scanned with a modern barcode at a checkout was a pack of Juicy Fruit gum in 1974.",
      "In the early 1970s American grocers agreed on a single standard, the Universal Product Code, or UPC. The winning design came from IBM, where Woodland was then working, and used straight vertical bars rather than a bullseye, which tended to smudge on fast printing presses. On 26 June 1974, at a Marsh supermarket in Troy, Ohio, a cashier scanned a ten-pack of Wrigley's Juicy Fruit gum, and the age of the scanner began. Take-up was slow at first, because shops needed expensive scanners and manufacturers had to print codes on everything. Once enough products carried them, the benefits snowballed: faster queues, fewer pricing errors and detailed data on exactly what sold and when. Europe soon adopted a compatible 13-digit system, which Australia uses too."],
    ["How a scanner reads stripes", "A scanner measures reflected light, and the widths of the dark and light bars encode the digits.",
      "A barcode is really a message written in light and shadow. The scanner shines light across the stripes, traditionally a red laser, although many modern scanners use a small camera instead. Black bars absorb most of the light and white spaces reflect it, so a sensor sees a rapidly changing pattern of bright and dark. Each digit is represented by a particular combination of bar and space widths. Guard patterns at the start, middle and end tell the scanner where the code begins and help it work out whether it is reading the code forwards or upside down. The number itself rarely contains the price. It identifies the product, and the shop's computer looks up the current price in its own database. That is why a price change does not mean relabelling every item."],
    ["A car factory needs more", "The QR code was invented in 1994 at Denso Wave to track car parts, inspired partly by the game of Go.",
      "An ordinary barcode holds only a short number, and in busy Japanese car factories workers were scanning many of them for every box of parts. At Denso Wave, then a division of the car-parts maker Denso, engineer Masahiro Hara led a small team to design something better. Hara has said the idea came to him while playing Go, whose black and white stones on a grid suggested storing information in two dimensions rather than one. Released in 1994, the Quick Response code could hold thousands of characters, including Japanese kanji. The team's hardest puzzle was making it instantly recognisable among the clutter of printed text. They studied printed material to find a shape that almost never appears by chance, and settled on the nested squares now sitting in three corners of every QR code."],
    ["Damaged but still readable", "QR codes use clever error correction, so they can still be read even when part of them is missing.",
      "Those three large squares are called finder patterns. Wherever the code sits in a camera image, and at whatever angle, the scanner spots them and works out which way is up. Smaller patterns help it correct for curves and tilt. The rest of the grid holds the data plus extra information for error correction, based on mathematics published by Irving Reed and Gustave Solomon in 1960, the same family of methods used on CDs and in communication with distant spacecraft. Whoever creates a QR code can choose one of four levels of protection. At the highest, roughly 30 per cent of the code can be dirty, torn or covered and the message can still be recovered. That is why some QR codes have a logo printed right over their middle and still scan perfectly."],
    ["Free to use, and easy to abuse", "Denso Wave chose not to enforce its patent, which helped QR codes spread, but they can also hide scams.",
      "Denso Wave held a patent on the QR code but announced it would not exercise it for codes following the published standard, so anyone could create and read them for free. That decision, plus smartphone cameras that could scan codes without a special app, turned QR codes into everyday objects. Australians scanned them millions of times to check in to venues during the COVID-19 pandemic, and they now appear on menus, parking meters and bills. The flip side is that a QR code is just a link you cannot read with your own eyes. Scammers stick fake codes over real ones or send them by email, leading to sites designed to steal card details or passwords. Check the web address before entering anything, and be wary of any code printed on a sticker."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "According to Norman Woodland's own account, what inspired the barcode?", ["The keys of a piano", "Morse code dots and dashes, drawn out in sand", "The rings of a tree", "Railway tracks seen from above"], 1, "Woodland recalled dragging his fingers through beach sand, stretching Morse code's dots and dashes into thin and thick lines."],
    [1, "What was the first item scanned with a UPC barcode at a supermarket checkout in 1974?", ["A can of soup", "A loaf of bread", "A ten-pack of Juicy Fruit gum", "A bottle of milk"], 2, "A cashier in Troy, Ohio, scanned a ten-pack of Wrigley's Juicy Fruit gum on 26 June 1974."],
    [2, "Why doesn't a shop have to relabel products when it changes a price?", ["The barcode only identifies the product; the price is looked up in the shop's database", "Scanners ignore price information", "Prices are printed in invisible ink", "Barcodes update themselves wirelessly"], 0, "The number in the barcode names the product, and the shop's computer supplies whatever the current price is."],
    [3, "What was the QR code originally designed to do?", ["Replace credit cards", "Track parts in car factories", "Store music", "Sort mail in post offices"], 1, "Denso Wave created it in 1994 to speed up the tracking of car parts, packing far more data than a barcode."],
    [4, "Why can a QR code with a logo over its centre still scan?", ["The logo is ignored by cameras", "Only the corners contain data", "Error correction lets the message be rebuilt even when part of the code is missing", "The logo itself contains the data"], 2, "Reed-Solomon error correction adds redundancy, so at the highest level about 30 per cent of the code can be lost."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("y2k-bug", "technology", "beginner", "Y2K: the disaster that didn't happen", "As 1999 drew to a close, the world braced for computers to think it was 1900. Midnight came and almost nothing broke. Was it all hype, or one of history's great quiet successes?",
  [
    ["Saving two digits", "Early programmers stored years as two digits to save scarce and expensive memory.",
      "In the 1960s and 1970s, computer memory and storage were astonishingly expensive, and a standard punch card had only 80 columns. Programmers saved space wherever they could, and one obvious shortcut was writing years as two digits: 68 instead of 1968. Everyone knew what it meant. Few expected their programs to still be running decades later, yet many were. Banking, payroll, airline and government systems had been built in layers on top of old code, often written in languages such as COBOL, and the originals were rarely replaced. By the 1990s, vast amounts of software assumed every year began with 19. When the calendar ticked over to 2000, the year 00 might be read as 1900. The problem became known as Y2K, short for Year 2000."],
    ["What could go wrong", "A two-digit year breaks any calculation that compares or subtracts dates across the century boundary.",
      "The fear was never that computers would explode. It was arithmetic. If a system calculates someone's age by subtracting their birth year from the current year, 00 minus 65 gives minus 65. Interest on a loan, the expiry date of a credit card, whether a warranty had lapsed, which records were old enough to delete: all relied on comparing dates. A computer sorting records might place the year 2000 before 1999. Some problems appeared years before the deadline, as systems began handling future dates such as card expiries and long-term contracts. There was a lesser-known complication too. Some programs failed to treat 2000 as a leap year, because the rule is fiddly: century years are normally not leap years, unless they are divisible by 400. The year 2000 was one."],
    ["Hidden in the machines", "The worry extended beyond office computers to chips embedded in equipment, though most turned out not to care about dates.",
      "Office computers were only part of the concern. Tiny processors were embedded in lifts, power stations, medical devices, factory controllers and building security systems, and nobody had a complete list of which ones tracked the date. Testing them was slow and costly. In practice, most embedded chips did not use calendar dates at all, or used them in harmless ways, but organisations could not know that without checking. That uncertainty is part of what made Y2K so unsettling. The fear was not one failure but many failures arriving at the same moment around the world, in systems connected to one another. Supply chains, utilities and banks all depend on each other, and a cascade of small faults could, in theory, have grown into something much bigger."],
    ["The great repair job", "Governments and businesses spent hundreds of billions of dollars checking and fixing code.",
      "Through the late 1990s, armies of programmers combed through old code, line by line, and retired COBOL programmers found themselves in demand again. Common fixes included expanding years to four digits, or 'windowing', where a program treats two-digit years below a cut-off, say 50, as 20xx and the rest as 19xx. Estimates of the worldwide cost vary widely, but are often put at somewhere between 300 and 600 billion US dollars. Australia, like many countries, ran national awareness and readiness programs, and governments set up command centres to monitor the changeover. Some critics argued that much of the money went to overcautious consultants. Others pointed out that the work also cleaned up and documented ageing systems that badly needed attention anyway."],
    ["Midnight", "When 2000 arrived, failures were few and minor, and Australia was among the first places to find out.",
      "Because of time zones, the Pacific and Australia reached midnight many hours before Europe and North America, and the world watched them for early signs of trouble. None came on a large scale. There were glitches. Some websites displayed the year as 19100, because of how certain programs added 1900 to the year, and a few ticketing and payment machines misbehaved. In Japan, some monitoring equipment at nuclear power plants reported errors, without any danger to safety. In the United States, a military satellite intelligence system had several hours of problems. Most faults were fixed quickly and passed almost unnoticed. Within days, some of the newspapers that had warned of chaos were running stories asking whether the whole thing had been a beat-up."],
    ["The preparedness paradox", "When prevention works, it can look as though there was never a problem in the first place.",
      "Y2K is a classic example of what is sometimes called the preparedness paradox. Fix a problem before it bites, and the absence of disaster can make the warning look silly. Experts still debate how bad things would have been without the effort. Some countries that spent relatively little also had few problems, partly because they relied less on old computer systems. Yet many people who did the work insist they found and fixed serious faults that would have caused real damage. Date bugs have not gone away, either. Many systems count time as seconds since 1 January 1970, and where that count is stored as a 32-bit number it will overflow on 19 January 2038. Much software has already been updated, but somewhere, quietly, the next deadline is ticking."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did early programs store years as two digits?", ["It was required by law", "To save scarce and expensive memory and storage", "Computers could not count past 99", "To make dates easier to read aloud"], 1, "Memory and storage were costly, so dropping the '19' saved space across millions of records."],
    [1, "Why was the year 2000 a tricky leap year for some software?", ["It was the first leap year ever recorded", "Century years are usually not leap years, but those divisible by 400 are", "It had two extra days", "Leap years were abolished in 1999"], 1, "The rule skips most century years, but 2000 is divisible by 400, so it was a leap year and some programs got it wrong."],
    [3, "What was 'windowing'?", ["Replacing old computers with new ones", "Running backup systems in parallel", "Treating two-digit years below a cut-off as 20xx and the rest as 19xx", "Shutting down systems over New Year"], 2, "Windowing was a cheaper fix than rewriting every date, interpreting two-digit years according to a chosen cut-off."],
    [4, "What odd year appeared on some websites on 1 January 2000?", ["1900", "2100", "0000", "19100"], 3, "Some programs added 1900 to a year counter that had reached 100 and then printed '19' in front of it."],
    [5, "What is the preparedness paradox?", ["Preparing too much always causes problems", "Successful prevention can make the original warning look exaggerated", "Nobody can prepare for computer failures", "Prepared people panic more"], 1, "Because the work prevented most failures, the lack of disaster led some to conclude the danger was never real."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("search-engines", "technology", "beginner", "How a search engine finds anything", "Type a few words and, in a fraction of a second, the right page appears out of billions. It is not searching the internet when you press Enter. It did that long before.",
  [
    ["A library with no catalogue", "The early web grew so fast that finding anything on it became a serious problem.",
      "When the World Wide Web opened to the public in the early 1990s, there were only a handful of websites, and people kept lists of them. Within a few years there were millions of pages, written by anyone, linked in every direction, with no central catalogue. Early helpers took two approaches. Yahoo began in 1994 as a hand-built directory, with people sorting sites into categories, rather like a library. Others, such as AltaVista, used software to read pages automatically and match the words you typed. Directories could not keep up with the web's explosive growth, and simple word-matching was easily fooled: a page that repeated a word hundreds of times could jump to the top. Search needed a better way to judge which pages were actually worth showing."],
    ["Crawlers: reading the web", "Search engines send out programs called crawlers that follow links from page to page and download what they find.",
      "Behind every search engine is a crawler, sometimes called a spider or a bot. It starts with a list of known web addresses, downloads each page, notes every link on it, and adds those new addresses to its list. Repeated endlessly, this lets a crawler explore much of the public web, though never all of it. Pages behind logins, private databases and sites that ask not to be crawled are left out. A website can put a small file called robots.txt on its server telling crawlers what they may visit, a convention dating back to 1994 that relies largely on good manners. Popular pages are revisited often, sometimes many times a day, while obscure ones may be checked only occasionally, so results can briefly lag behind reality."],
    ["The index", "Instead of searching the web when you type, a search engine looks things up in a giant prepared index, like the one at the back of a book.",
      "A common assumption is that a search engine scans the internet the moment you press Enter. It doesn't; that would take far too long. Instead it has already built an index, organised a little like the index at the back of a textbook. For every word, it stores a list of the pages that contain it, along with details such as where on the page the word appears. When you search for 'koala habitat', the engine pulls up the list for each word and finds pages that appear on both. Computer scientists call this an inverted index. Google's is spread across huge numbers of computers in data centres around the world and runs to well over a hundred million gigabytes, which is why answers return in a fraction of a second."],
    ["Links as votes", "Google's founders ranked pages by the links pointing to them, treating each link as a kind of vote.",
      "In 1996, two Stanford PhD students, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, began a research project nicknamed BackRub. Their insight came from academic publishing, where an important paper is one that many other papers cite. They treated a link from one web page to another as a citation. A page linked to by lots of other pages was probably useful, and a link from an important page counted for more than a link from an obscure one. They called the measure PageRank, a pun on Page's surname. Their search engine, launched as a company called Google in 1998, took its name from 'googol', the number 1 followed by 100 zeros, with a change of spelling. Its results were noticeably better than its rivals', and within a few years it dominated web search."],
    ["Ranking today", "Modern search ranking blends many signals, including meaning, quality, freshness and your location.",
      "PageRank still matters, but it is now one ingredient among many. Search engines try to work out what you meant, not just what you typed, so a search for 'how tall is Uluru' can surface a page that says 'Uluru rises 348 metres above the plain'. They consider how recent a page is, whether it works well on phones, the language you use and where you are searching from, which is why 'pizza near me' gives different answers in Perth and in Hobart. Increasingly, machine learning models help interpret meaning. Exactly how the signals are combined is a closely guarded secret, partly for competitive reasons and partly to stop people gaming the system. A whole industry, search engine optimisation, exists to help websites climb the rankings."],
    ["Who pays for free search", "Most search engines are free to use because they earn money from advertising matched to what you are looking for.",
      "Running a search engine costs a fortune, yet you pay nothing to use one. The money comes mainly from advertising. When you search for 'running shoes', advertisers compete in an automated auction, run in a split second, for the chance to show you a link, and they typically pay only when someone clicks. A search is a powerful moment for advertisers, because you are telling the engine exactly what you want right now. This business model has been enormously profitable, and it explains a few things worth knowing. Sponsored results often appear above ordinary ones, marked with a small label. Search engines also learn from what people click. It pays to notice the difference between an ad and a result, and to remember that the top link is not automatically the best one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did hand-built web directories like early Yahoo struggle?", ["They charged users to search", "They could not keep up with how fast the web was growing", "They only worked in English", "They were banned by governments"], 1, "People sorting sites by hand simply could not keep pace with millions of new pages."],
    [1, "What is robots.txt?", ["A virus that attacks search engines", "A list of the world's robots", "A file where a website tells crawlers what they may visit", "The code inside a search box"], 2, "Dating from 1994, it is a polite convention that well-behaved crawlers follow."],
    [2, "What actually happens when you press Enter on a search?", ["The engine looks up your words in an index it built earlier", "The engine visits every website live", "Your computer downloads the whole web", "A person finds the answer for you"], 0, "Searching the live web would be far too slow, so engines consult a huge inverted index prepared in advance."],
    [3, "What was the key idea behind PageRank?", ["Ranking pages by how often they repeat a word", "Ranking pages alphabetically", "Ranking pages by how new they are", "Treating links from other pages as votes, with important pages' votes counting more"], 3, "Borrowed from academic citations, it treated links as signs that a page was worth reading."],
    [5, "How do most search engines make their money?", ["Selling search results to governments", "Advertising matched to what people search for", "Charging websites to be crawled", "Subscription fees from users"], 1, "Advertisers bid to appear for particular searches, usually paying when someone clicks."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("mobile-networks", "technology", "beginner", "How a mobile call finds you", "Your phone is a radio, quietly chatting with towers you never notice. Here is how a call reaches you, even on a moving train.",
  [
    ["A phone is a two-way radio", "Mobile phones send and receive radio waves, talking to a nearby base station rather than directly to each other.",
      "When you call a friend across the street, your two phones do not talk directly. Your phone sends radio signals to the nearest base station, usually a set of antennas on a tower, rooftop or pole. From there the call travels over cables and optical fibre through the operator's network, and back out to whichever base station is near your friend. Radio is only the first and last link in the chain. Phones transmit at fairly low power to save battery, so networks need base stations spread across the landscape, often only a few hundred metres apart in busy city areas. The central problem engineers had to solve was how to let millions of people share a limited range of radio frequencies at the same time without drowning each other out."],
    ["Cells and honeycombs", "Dividing the land into small cells lets the same radio frequencies be reused again and again.",
      "The answer, proposed at Bell Labs in 1947 by engineers Douglas Ring and W. Rae Young, was to divide an area into small zones called cells, each with its own base station. Neighbouring cells use different frequencies, but cells far enough apart can reuse the same ones without interfering. Planners often draw them as a honeycomb of hexagons, although real cells are messy shapes bent by hills and buildings. The clever part is that capacity grows as cells shrink. A busy city centre may have many small cells, while a rural area may be covered by a few large ones. This is why the technology is called cellular, and why Americans call their phones cell phones. The idea took decades to become practical, waiting on electronics able to manage it."],
    ["Handover on the move", "As you travel, the network passes your call from one cell to the next, usually without you noticing.",
      "Cells only work if a call can survive moving between them. Your phone constantly measures the strength of signals from nearby base stations and reports back. As you drive away from one tower and towards another, the network decides when to switch and moves your call to the new cell in a fraction of a second. This is called handover. On a fast train it can happen every minute or so. When there is no neighbouring cell to take over, as on a remote highway, the call drops. Even when you are not on a call, an idle phone checks in from time to time, so the network knows roughly which group of cells you are in. That is how an incoming call knows where to find you."],
    ["What the SIM does", "Your SIM securely proves to the network who you are, using a secret key that never leaves the chip.",
      "The little SIM card, or its built-in digital version, the eSIM, is a tiny secure computer. It stores an identity number linked to your account and a secret key that is designed never to leave the chip. When your phone connects, the network sends a random challenge. The SIM combines it with the secret key to produce an answer, and the network, which holds a copy of the same key, checks the result. If it matches, you are in. This lets the network bill the right person and makes it hard to impersonate someone else's phone. Older analogue phones had no such protection: their calls could be overheard with a radio scanner and their identities copied, or cloned, to run up bills on someone else's account."],
    ["From 1G to 5G", "Each mobile generation brought a big change, from analogue voice to texting to fast mobile internet.",
      "The first generation, 1G, arrived in the 1980s and carried voice as analogue radio signals; Australia's first cellular network opened in 1987. 2G, in the early 1990s, went digital, which improved security and made room for text messages. The first text was sent on 3 December 1992, when engineer Neil Papworth typed 'Merry Christmas' from a computer to a colleague's phone in Britain. Messages were capped at 160 characters, a limit that shaped how a generation wrote. 3G, from the early 2000s, made mobile internet practical, and 4G, from around 2010, made video streaming and apps routine. 5G adds higher speeds and shorter delays, partly by using higher frequencies with a shorter range, which means many more small cells."],
    ["Old networks switch off", "Older networks are shut down to free up radio spectrum, which is why old phones can stop working.",
      "Radio spectrum is limited, so operators periodically switch off old networks to reuse their frequencies for newer, more efficient ones. Australia closed its analogue network in 2000, its 2G networks during the 2010s, and its 3G networks in 2023 and 2024. Some older phones that relied on 3G for voice calls suddenly could not call anyone, including emergency services. If you or an older relative are unsure whether a phone still works properly, your provider can check it. In an emergency, call 000 from any mobile. If your own network has no coverage but another network does, your emergency call can be carried on that other network. The number 112 also works from mobiles, but 000 remains Australia's main emergency number."]
  ],
  [
    [1, "Why are mobile networks divided into many small cells?", ["So that phones can talk directly to each other", "So the same frequencies can be reused in cells far enough apart", "To make towers cheaper to paint", "Because radio only travels in hexagons"], 1, "Reusing frequencies across non-neighbouring cells lets millions of people share limited spectrum."],
    [2, "What is 'handover'?", ["Passing your phone to someone else", "Switching from mobile data to Wi-Fi", "Moving an ongoing call from one cell to the next as you travel", "Transferring your number to a new provider"], 2, "The network switches your call to a stronger base station, usually in a fraction of a second."],
    [3, "How does a SIM prove your identity to the network?", ["By sending your name and address", "By answering a random challenge using a secret key stored on the chip", "By sharing your fingerprint", "By sending a photo of the phone's owner"], 1, "Both the SIM and the network hold the same key, so a correct answer proves the SIM is genuine."],
    [4, "What did the first text message, sent in 1992, say?", ["Hello world", "Merry Christmas", "Can you hear me?", "Happy New Year"], 1, "Engineer Neil Papworth sent 'Merry Christmas' to a colleague's phone on 3 December 1992."],
    [5, "Why do operators switch off older networks such as 3G?", ["Old networks are illegal", "To free up limited radio spectrum for newer, more efficient technology", "Because phone towers wear out every ten years", "To force people to change providers"], 1, "Spectrum is scarce, so old networks are retired and their frequencies reused for 4G and 5G."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("how-screens-work", "technology", "beginner", "How screens make pictures", "Everything on your screen, from a sunset photo to this sentence, is built from millions of tiny lights in just three colours.",
  [
    ["Millions of tiny dots", "A screen is a grid of pixels, short for picture elements, each set to its own colour many times a second.",
      "Look at a screen through a magnifying glass, or through a drop of water on a phone, and the illusion falls apart. The smooth image is really a grid of tiny dots called pixels, short for picture elements. A 4K television has 3840 pixels across and 2160 down, more than eight million in all. Each pixel shows one colour at a time, and the screen's electronics update every pixel many times per second. Your eyes and brain blend the grid into a continuous picture and the rapid updates into smooth motion. The closer together the pixels are packed, the sharper the image looks. A modern phone fits several hundred pixels into every inch, so densely that at normal viewing distance the individual dots are invisible."],
    ["Three colours make every colour", "Each pixel mixes red, green and blue light, and your eyes blend them into the full range of colours.",
      "Each pixel is usually made of three smaller subpixels: one red, one green and one blue. By varying their brightness, a screen can produce millions of colours. Red and green light together look yellow, all three at full strength look white, and all three off look black. This is additive colour mixing, the opposite of mixing paints, where adding more colours makes things darker. It works because of the way our eyes are built. The retina has three types of cone cells, most sensitive to long, medium and short wavelengths of light. A screen does not reproduce the real light coming off a lemon, only a mix of red, green and blue that stimulates our cones in the same proportions. Animals with different eyes would likely see our screens quite differently."],
    ["LCD: light through a shutter", "An LCD screen shines a constant backlight through liquid crystals that act as tiny adjustable shutters.",
      "Most computer monitors and many televisions use liquid crystal displays, or LCDs. Behind the panel is a backlight, today usually made of LEDs, that shines white light the whole time. In front of it sit two polarising filters, set at angles to each other, with a thin layer of liquid crystal in between. Liquid crystals are substances whose molecules can flow like a liquid yet line up in an orderly way, like a crystal. A small voltage twists or untwists them, changing how much light makes it through the second filter. Each subpixel has its own colour filter and its own tiny transistor to control it. The weakness is that the backlight stays on behind everything, so dark scenes can look slightly grey rather than truly black."],
    ["OLED: pixels that glow", "In an OLED screen every subpixel makes its own light, so a black pixel can simply switch off.",
      "OLED stands for organic light-emitting diode. Here there is no backlight. Each subpixel is a thin sandwich of carbon-based materials that glow when an electric current passes through them. To show black, a pixel simply turns off, giving deep blacks and striking contrast, and it can save battery on phones when much of the screen is dark. OLED panels can also be thinner and even flexible, which is what makes folding phones possible. The trade-offs are cost and ageing. The organic materials slowly lose brightness with use, and blue ones fade fastest. If the same image stays on screen for very long periods, such as a news ticker or a game's scoreboard, it can leave a faint permanent ghost, known as burn-in. Manufacturers fight it with tricks such as subtly shifting the image."],
    ["Frames per second", "Screens redraw the whole image many times a second, and this refresh rate is measured in hertz.",
      "A screen never shows true motion. It shows a rapid series of still images, each slightly different, like a flipbook. The number of times it redraws each second is the refresh rate, measured in hertz. Many screens refresh 60 times a second, while newer phones, gaming monitors and televisions reach 120 hertz or more, which makes scrolling and fast games feel noticeably smoother. Films are traditionally shot at only 24 frames per second, so a screen often shows each frame of a movie more than once. Some phones change their refresh rate on the fly, dropping as low as one hertz for a still image to save battery, then jumping back up as soon as you start to scroll. The faster your finger moves, the more those extra frames matter."],
    ["Resolution and the eye", "Beyond a certain pixel density, extra resolution makes little visible difference at normal viewing distances.",
      "More pixels are not always better. Whether you can see the difference depends on pixel size, screen size and how far away you are. The human eye can only resolve detail down to a certain fineness, and once pixels are smaller than that at your viewing distance, adding more brings little visible gain. Apple built its 'Retina' marketing in 2010 around exactly this idea: pixels too small to pick out individually at a typical viewing distance. It also explains why, for most people watching from the couch, an 8K television offers only a modest improvement over 4K. Other qualities, such as contrast, brightness, colour accuracy and how cleanly the screen handles fast motion, often matter more to how good a picture actually looks."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the word 'pixel' stand for?", ["Picture element", "Pixel light", "Photo cell", "Primary exel"], 0, "Pixel is short for picture element, the smallest dot a screen controls individually."],
    [1, "On a screen, what do red and green light combine to make?", ["Brown", "Yellow", "Purple", "Black"], 1, "Screens use additive mixing of light, where red plus green looks yellow, unlike mixing paints."],
    [2, "Why can dark scenes look slightly grey on an LCD?", ["The pixels are too large", "The liquid crystals glow faintly", "The backlight stays on behind every pixel, and some leaks through", "The screen refreshes too slowly"], 2, "LCDs block a constant backlight, and the crystals can never block it completely."],
    [3, "What is burn-in on an OLED screen?", ["The screen overheating", "A faint permanent ghost left by an image shown for very long periods", "A cracked display", "Colours becoming too bright"], 1, "The organic materials age with use, so pixels showing the same image for ages wear unevenly."],
    [4, "What does a 120 hertz refresh rate mean?", ["The screen uses 120 watts", "The screen has 120 colours", "The screen redraws its image 120 times per second", "The screen lasts 120 months"], 2, "Hertz means times per second, so the image is updated 120 times each second for smoother motion."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("transistor-story", "technology", "intermediate", "The transistor: the switch that changed everything", "In 1947 two physicists built a clumsy contraption of gold foil, plastic and germanium. Its descendants now number in the billions inside your phone.",
  [
    ["The trouble with valves", "Before transistors, electronics relied on vacuum tubes, which were hot, bulky and failed often.",
      "In the first half of the twentieth century, electronics meant vacuum tubes, known in Australia and Britain as valves. These glass bulbs could amplify a signal or switch a current on and off, making radio, television and the first electronic computers possible. But they were bulky and power hungry, they ran hot, and their filaments burned out like light globes. ENIAC, an American computer completed in 1945, used around 17,500 of them, filled a large room and drew about 150 kilowatts of power. Tubes failed regularly, and finding the dead one took time. The American telephone giant AT&T had a particular interest in something better, because its network relied on huge numbers of tubes and mechanical switches. Its research arm, Bell Labs, went looking for a solid replacement."],
    ["Christmas at Bell Labs", "In December 1947, John Bardeen and Walter Brattain demonstrated the first working transistor.",
      "Bell Labs assembled a team to study semiconductors, materials such as germanium and silicon that conduct electricity better than an insulator but worse than a metal. In December 1947, physicists John Bardeen and Walter Brattain pressed two closely spaced gold contacts onto a slab of germanium and found that a small current through one could control a larger current through the other. The device amplified a signal. They demonstrated it to Bell Labs managers on 23 December. It was an awkward-looking thing, held together with a plastic wedge and a spring, but it worked. Their group leader, William Shockley, was reportedly frustrated not to have been directly involved, and within weeks he had worked out a sturdier design, the junction transistor, which became the basis for practical devices."],
    ["Why a switch matters", "A transistor is a tiny electrically controlled switch, and switches are all a computer needs to calculate.",
      "A transistor does two related jobs. It can amplify, turning a weak signal into a stronger copy, which made pocket transistor radios possible. And it can act as a switch with no moving parts, where a small voltage on one terminal turns a larger current on or off. That second job is the foundation of computing. Computers represent information as on and off, one and zero. Combine a few transistors and you get logic gates, circuits whose output is on only if both inputs are on, or if either is. Combine gates and you can add numbers, compare them and store them. Because transistors can switch billions of times per second while using very little power, enormous numbers of them can work together on a single chip."],
    ["A Nobel, and a walkout", "The inventors shared a Nobel Prize, but Shockley's difficult management helped spark the birth of Silicon Valley.",
      "Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics. By then Shockley had left Bell Labs and set up the Shockley Semiconductor Laboratory in Mountain View, California, hiring brilliant young scientists. His management proved so suspicious and erratic that in 1957 eight of them walked out together. They became known as the 'traitorous eight'. With backing from an industrial company, they founded Fairchild Semiconductor, which became a seedbed for the region later nicknamed Silicon Valley. Two of the eight, Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore, went on to found Intel in 1968. Bardeen, meanwhile, won a second Nobel Prize in Physics in 1972, for helping explain superconductivity, and remains the only person to have won that prize twice."],
    ["Putting it all on one chip", "The integrated circuit, invented independently by Jack Kilby and Robert Noyce, put many components on a single piece of material.",
      "Early transistors were separate components, soldered by hand into circuits. As designs grew more complex, the wiring itself became the problem, a challenge engineers called the tyranny of numbers. In 1958, Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments built a working circuit with its components formed in a single piece of germanium. Months later, Robert Noyce at Fairchild proposed a more practical approach in silicon, building on the flat 'planar' process developed by his colleague Jean Hoerni, which let components and their connections be made together, rather like printing. Both men are credited as inventors of the integrated circuit. Kilby received a share of the 2000 Nobel Prize in Physics for it; Noyce had died in 1990. The microchip turned electronics from a craft into something closer to mass printing."],
    ["Moore's prediction", "Gordon Moore predicted in 1965 that the number of transistors on a chip would keep doubling, and for decades he was right.",
      "In 1965 Gordon Moore, then at Fairchild, wrote a magazine article observing that the number of components on a chip had been doubling every year, and predicted the trend would continue for at least a decade. In 1975 he revised the pace to roughly every two years. This became known as Moore's law, though it is an observation about engineering and economics rather than a law of nature. It held up remarkably well for about half a century, partly because the industry treated it as a target to hit. Modern chips contain tens of billions of transistors, each far smaller than a bacterium. Progress has slowed as some parts of transistors shrink to just a few atoms thick, and engineers now debate how much longer the trend can continue."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the main problem with vacuum tubes?", ["They could only be made in Japan", "They were bulky, ran hot, used lots of power and burned out", "They could not amplify signals", "They only worked underwater"], 1, "Tubes did the job, but machines like ENIAC needed thousands of them, with huge power use and frequent failures."],
    [1, "Who demonstrated the first working transistor in December 1947?", ["Thomas Edison and Nikola Tesla", "Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore", "John Bardeen and Walter Brattain", "Jack Kilby and Jean Hoerni"], 2, "Bardeen and Brattain's point-contact transistor was shown to Bell Labs managers on 23 December 1947."],
    [2, "Why is a transistor's ability to act as a switch so important for computers?", ["Switches make computers quieter", "Computers represent information as on and off, and switches combine into logic gates", "Switches stop computers overheating", "It lets computers connect to the internet"], 1, "On-off switches build logic gates, and gates build circuits that add, compare and store numbers."],
    [3, "What did the 'traitorous eight' found after leaving Shockley?", ["Bell Labs", "IBM", "Texas Instruments", "Fairchild Semiconductor"], 3, "Fairchild became a seedbed of Silicon Valley, and two of its founders later started Intel."],
    [5, "What is Moore's law?", ["A law of physics about electricity", "An observation that the number of transistors on chips roughly doubles every two years", "A rule that computers must halve in price yearly", "A law banning chips smaller than a virus"], 1, "It began as Gordon Moore's 1965 observation and became an industry target, not a law of nature."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("phone-cameras", "technology", "intermediate", "How your phone camera takes a photo", "A phone camera's lens is smaller than a pea, yet it can rival cameras costing thousands of dollars. The secret is less about glass and more about software.",
  [
    ["Catching light in buckets", "A camera sensor is a grid of millions of light-sensitive sites that turn light into electric charge.",
      "Where film cameras used chemistry, a digital camera uses an image sensor, a silicon chip covered in millions of tiny light-sensitive sites, often called photosites. Each works a little like a bucket left out in the rain. During an exposure, particles of light called photons strike the silicon and knock electrons loose, and the photosite collects them. Brighter light means more photons and more charge. When the exposure ends, the charge in each bucket is measured and converted into a number. That grid of numbers is the raw material of your photo. Almost all phone cameras use a type of sensor called CMOS, built with the same manufacturing methods as computer chips, which made sensors cheap and power-efficient enough to fit inside phones."],
    ["Colour from a checkerboard", "Sensors only measure brightness, so a mosaic of coloured filters, the Bayer filter, is used to capture colour.",
      "A photosite cannot tell what colour light it receives; it only counts how much. To capture colour, most sensors place a tiny filter over each photosite, letting through only red, green or blue light. The usual arrangement, patented in 1976 by Bryce Bayer, an engineer at Kodak, is a checkerboard pattern with twice as many green filters as red or blue, because our eyes are most sensitive to detail in green light. So each photosite knows about only one colour. Software then estimates the two missing colours at every point by looking at its neighbours, a process called demosaicing. In a sense, two-thirds of the colour information in a typical digital photo is educated guesswork, though the guesses are very good indeed."],
    ["The small-sensor problem", "Tiny sensors collect less light, which makes photos noisy in dim conditions.",
      "Phones are thin, so their sensors and lenses must be small. A full-frame professional camera sensor is about 36 by 24 millimetres; most phone sensors are a fraction of that area. A smaller sensor gathers less light overall, and in a dim room each photosite may collect only a small number of photons. Light arrives randomly, so with few photons the counts fluctuate from one photosite to the next, and the image fills with grainy speckles called noise. The electronics add a little noise of their own. This is a physical limit, not a manufacturing fault, and it explains why phone photos used to look so poor indoors and at night. Cramming in more megapixels can make it worse, because each photosite becomes smaller still."],
    ["Many photos in one", "Modern phones quietly capture a burst of frames and merge them into a single better image.",
      "The breakthrough was computational photography. When you press the shutter, a modern phone has often already been capturing frames continuously, and it combines several of them into one picture. Averaging many frames cancels out much of the random noise, in the same way that averaging many people's guesses tends to cancel out individual errors. Combining shorter and longer exposures lets the phone keep detail in both a bright sky and dark shadows, a technique called high dynamic range, or HDR. Night modes go further, capturing frames over a second or more and aligning them to correct for your shaking hands. All of this happens in moments on dedicated processors. So the photo you see is not a single instant of light, but a carefully assembled composite."],
    ["Software that makes choices", "Phones also use software to sharpen, smooth, adjust colours and simulate effects such as blurred backgrounds.",
      "After merging frames, the phone keeps editing. It adjusts white balance so that a white shirt looks white under orange indoor lighting, sharpens edges, smooths skin and boosts colours to look pleasing. Many phones recognise what is in the scene, such as food, faces or sky, and treat each part differently. Portrait mode imitates the soft background blur of a large camera lens. A phone's tiny lens produces little natural blur, so software estimates how far away each part of the scene is, using two cameras, depth sensors or machine learning, then blurs the background artificially. Look closely at hair or the edges of glasses and you can sometimes spot mistakes. Different brands make different choices, which is why the same scene can look noticeably different on two phones."],
    ["Is it still a photograph?", "Because phones interpret scenes so heavily, a photo is part record and part reconstruction.",
      "All cameras interpret the world. Film chemistry favoured some colours over others, and photographers have always chosen exposure and framing. But phones push interpretation much further, and the line between capturing and creating has blurred. Some features can erase unwanted people from the background, or combine the best expressions from several shots of a group. A well-known controversy arose in 2023 over phone photos of the Moon, when a tester showed that one brand's processing added lunar detail that was missing from a deliberately blurred original, by drawing on a model trained on what the Moon looks like. Most processing is harmless and simply produces better pictures. Still, a photo from a modern phone is best thought of as the camera's best estimate of a scene, not a perfect copy."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does each photosite on a camera sensor actually measure?", ["The colour of the light", "The distance to the subject", "How much light, by collecting electrons knocked loose by photons", "The temperature of the scene"], 2, "Photosites count light like buckets collecting rain; colour has to be added by filters."],
    [1, "Why does the Bayer filter use twice as many green filters as red or blue?", ["Green filters are cheaper", "Our eyes are most sensitive to detail in green light", "Green light travels faster", "Most scenes are mostly green"], 1, "Bryce Bayer's pattern favours green to match how human vision picks up detail."],
    [2, "Why do photos from small sensors look grainy in dim light?", ["The lens gets dirty in the dark", "Few photons arrive, so random variation between photosites shows up as noise", "Phones deliberately add grain", "Sensors stop working below a certain temperature"], 1, "With little light, the natural randomness of photon arrival becomes visible as speckled noise."],
    [3, "How does combining many frames improve a phone photo?", ["It makes the file smaller", "Averaging frames cancels out much of the random noise", "It increases the number of pixels on the sensor", "It makes the lens wider"], 1, "Random noise differs between frames, so averaging them leaves the real image clearer."],
    [4, "How does a phone create portrait mode's blurred background?", ["Its lens is wider than a professional camera's", "It estimates depth and blurs the background with software", "It takes the photo out of focus on purpose", "It uses a special blurry filter over the lens"], 1, "Small phone lenses give little natural blur, so software maps depth and adds the blur afterwards."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("text-encoding", "technology", "intermediate", "How computers store letters and emoji", "Computers only understand numbers. So how does a letter, a Chinese character or a laughing-crying face survive the trip from your phone to someone else's?",
  [
    ["Every letter is a number", "Computers store text by giving every character an agreed number, a system called a character encoding.",
      "Inside a computer there are no letters, only binary numbers. To store text, everyone involved must agree on a table that maps each character to a number. Send the number 65 and the receiving computer, using the same table, shows a capital A. Early computers each had their own tables, so text from one machine could turn to gibberish on another. In 1963, American standards bodies published the first version of ASCII, the American Standard Code for Information Interchange, refined over the following years. It used 7 bits, allowing 128 codes: upper and lower case English letters, digits, punctuation and control codes, such as a 'bell' code that rang a bell on teleprinters. ASCII was a huge success, but it was designed for English and left out most of the world's writing."],
    ["Babel on the screen", "Rival encodings for other languages led to garbled text, a problem known by the Japanese word mojibake.",
      "Computers usually store data in bytes of eight bits, which can hold 256 values, so many systems used the spare 128 slots for accented letters, symbols or other alphabets. The trouble was that different countries and companies filled those slots differently. Greek, Russian, Hebrew and Western European text each had their own versions, and languages such as Chinese and Japanese, with thousands of characters, needed more complex schemes of their own. Open a document using the wrong table and the numbers came out as the wrong characters. Japanese users had a word for the resulting jumble: mojibake, roughly 'character transformation'. Anyone who has seen an apostrophe turn into a string like â€™ on a web page has met the same problem."],
    ["One code for every script", "Unicode set out to give every character in every writing system its own unique number.",
      "In the late 1980s, engineers including Joe Becker of Xerox and Lee Collins and Mark Davis of Apple began working on a universal solution. The Unicode Consortium was founded in 1991 and published the first version of the standard that year. Its goal was simple to state and enormous to achieve: one number, called a code point, for every character in every writing system, past and present. Capital A is still 65, to stay compatible with ASCII, and is written in Unicode as U+0041. Unicode now covers well over 150,000 characters across more than 160 scripts, including Arabic, Devanagari, Cherokee, Egyptian hieroglyphs and cuneiform. New characters are added through a formal proposal process, and the standard is updated roughly once a year."],
    ["A design on a placemat", "UTF-8, sketched in a diner in 1992, became the way most text on the web is stored.",
      "Giving characters numbers is only half the job; you also need a way to store those numbers as bytes. One evening in September 1992, computer scientists Ken Thompson and Rob Pike, then working at Bell Labs, sketched a scheme on a placemat in a New Jersey diner. It became UTF-8. Its elegance is that ordinary English text is stored exactly as in ASCII, one byte per character, so old files and programs keep working. Other characters use two, three or four bytes, with bit patterns that clearly mark where each character starts, so software never mistakes part of one character for another. UTF-8 spread steadily, and today the great majority of web pages use it, a quiet but enormous victory for compatibility."],
    ["Emoji arrive", "Emoji began on Japanese mobile phones in the late 1990s and entered Unicode in 2010.",
      "Emoji come from Japan, where the word combines e, meaning picture, and moji, meaning character; its resemblance to the English word 'emotion' is a coincidence. Japanese mobile phones offered picture characters in the late 1990s. A set of 176 simple designs, each 12 by 12 pixels, created by Shigetaka Kurita for NTT Docomo's mobile internet service in 1999, became the most famous early collection and is now held by the Museum of Modern Art in New York, although another carrier had offered picture symbols slightly earlier. Each Japanese carrier used its own codes, so emoji often broke when sent between networks. Pressure from companies including Google and Apple led to emoji being added to Unicode in 2010, which let them spread worldwide."],
    ["Same code, different picture", "Unicode defines what an emoji means, but each company draws its own version, and some are built from combinations.",
      "Unicode assigns each emoji a code point and a name, such as U+1F602, 'face with tears of joy', but it does not dictate the artwork. Apple, Google, Microsoft, Samsung and others draw their own designs, so a message can look subtly different on the recipient's phone. Designs are sometimes redrawn: the pistol emoji, for example, was changed to a toy water gun by most major platforms in the late 2010s. Many emoji are not single characters at all. Skin tones are added by attaching a modifier to a base emoji, and many family and profession emoji are built by joining several emoji with an invisible character called a zero-width joiner. A single small picture on your screen can be made of seven or more code points."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How many different codes did 7-bit ASCII allow?", ["26", "128", "256", "1,000"], 1, "Seven bits give 2 to the power of 7, or 128, possible codes, enough for English but little else."],
    [1, "What is 'mojibake'?", ["A Japanese emoji set", "Garbled text produced when the wrong character encoding is used", "A type of computer virus", "A font for writing kanji"], 1, "Reading bytes with the wrong table turns text into a jumble, which Japanese users named mojibake."],
    [3, "What makes UTF-8 so practical?", ["It stores every character in exactly one byte", "It only works for English", "Plain English text is stored exactly as in ASCII, so old files keep working", "It compresses images"], 2, "UTF-8 is backwards compatible with ASCII while still able to represent every Unicode character."],
    [4, "What does the Japanese word 'emoji' mean?", ["Emotion icon", "Picture character", "Small face", "Mobile message"], 1, "E means picture and moji means character; the link to 'emotion' is a coincidence."],
    [5, "How are many family and profession emoji created?", ["By joining several emoji with an invisible zero-width joiner", "By drawing them by hand on each phone", "By sending a photo instead of text", "By using a separate app"], 0, "Several code points glued by zero-width joiners are displayed as a single picture."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("open-source", "technology", "intermediate", "Open source: the free code running the world", "Much of the digital world runs on software written in the open and given away. In 2024, one engineer's curiosity about a half-second delay showed both the strength and the fragility of that system.",
  [
    ["Recipes anyone can read", "Open source software shares its source code so anyone can read, change and redistribute it.",
      "Software begins as source code, instructions written by people in a programming language. Most commercial programs are distributed only in compiled form, which computers can run but humans can barely read, so the recipe stays secret. Open source software publishes the recipe. Anyone can study how it works, fix it, improve it or build something new from it, under a licence that sets out the rules. Some licences let you do almost anything; others require that if you share a modified version, you must share your changes too. Open source does not necessarily mean nobody is paid. Many developers are employed by companies that rely on the software. But the code itself is shared, and it is everywhere: phones, cars, televisions, routers and the servers behind most websites."],
    ["A printer that started a movement", "Richard Stallman launched the free software movement in 1983, arguing that users should control their programs.",
      "In computing's early days, researchers shared code freely, but by the late 1970s software was increasingly locked up as a commercial product. Richard Stallman, a programmer at the MIT Artificial Intelligence Laboratory, later described a formative frustration: a new laser printer kept jamming, and he could not fix the problem in its software because the maker would not share the source code. In 1983 he announced the GNU project, to build a complete operating system made only of free software, and in 1985 he founded the Free Software Foundation. 'Free' meant freedom, not price, which he summed up as free as in free speech, not free beer. His GNU General Public License required shared modifications to stay free, a clever legal twist nicknamed copyleft."],
    ["Just a hobby", "In 1991 a Finnish student, Linus Torvalds, started the Linux kernel, which completed a free operating system.",
      "By the early 1990s, the GNU project had built many tools but still lacked a finished kernel, the core of an operating system that manages memory, processors and hardware. In August 1991, Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old student at the University of Helsinki, posted a message online saying he was writing a free operating system, adding that it was just a hobby and would not be big and professional like GNU. He released the code, and programmers around the world began sending improvements. Combined with GNU's tools, Linux became a complete free operating system. Torvalds later created Git as well, now the most widely used tool for tracking changes to code. Few hobbies have turned out quite so big and professional."],
    ["Running the world", "Linux and other open source software now underpin phones, servers, supercomputers and the web.",
      "Today Linux is far more widespread than most people realise. Android, the world's most popular phone operating system, is built on the Linux kernel. Linux runs most of the servers behind websites and cloud services, and since 2017 every one of the world's 500 fastest supercomputers on the regularly published TOP500 list has run it. Beyond Linux, open source projects supply web browser engines, databases, programming languages and the encryption libraries that help protect online banking. Companies large and small build on this shared foundation rather than writing everything from scratch, which saves enormous effort. Even firms that once treated open source as a threat came around. Microsoft's chief executive Steve Ballmer called Linux 'a cancer' in 2001; Microsoft later became one of open source's biggest contributors."],
    ["Holding up the internet", "Critical open source projects are sometimes maintained by tiny, underfunded teams, which creates hidden risks.",
      "Shared code has a weakness: everyone uses it, but no one in particular is responsible for it. In 2014 researchers discovered Heartbleed, a serious flaw in OpenSSL, encryption software used by a large share of the web's secure servers. It emerged that the project was run by a handful of people, with only one working on it full time, and received only around 2000 US dollars a year in donations. In 2021 another severe flaw, Log4Shell, turned up in Log4j, a widely used tool for recording what programs do, again maintained largely by volunteers. Each crisis pushed companies and governments to fund critical projects more seriously. A popular web cartoon sums up the worry: modern digital infrastructure balanced on one small project that a lone volunteer has been thanklessly maintaining for years."],
    ["Half a second that saved the internet", "In 2024 an engineer noticed a tiny slowdown and uncovered a backdoor planted in widely used open source software.",
      "In March 2024, Andres Freund, a Microsoft software engineer who works on the PostgreSQL database, noticed something odd: logging in to a test machine was using more processor time than it should, adding about half a second. Curious, he traced the delay to xz Utils, a small compression tool included in many Linux systems. Someone had hidden a backdoor in it that could have let an attacker take control of affected servers. The culprit, using the name Jia Tan, had spent around two years earning the trust of the project's overstretched volunteer maintainer before gaining the access needed to slip it in. It was caught before reaching the stable versions of major Linux distributions. Who was behind it remains unknown, though many security experts suspect a well-resourced, possibly state-backed group."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes software 'open source'?", ["It is always free of charge and written by volunteers", "Its source code is published so anyone can read, change and share it under a licence", "It has no bugs", "It only runs on Linux"], 1, "Open source is about access to the source code; many contributors are actually paid by companies."],
    [1, "What did Richard Stallman mean by 'free' software?", ["Software that costs nothing", "Software with no licence", "Freedom for users to run, study, change and share it", "Software made by the government"], 2, "His slogan 'free as in free speech, not free beer' stressed freedom rather than price."],
    [2, "How did Linus Torvalds describe his project in 1991?", ["As the future of all computing", "As a commercial rival to Microsoft", "As just a hobby that would not be big and professional", "As a secret military project"], 2, "His modest announcement became one of the most widely used pieces of software ever written."],
    [4, "What did the Heartbleed bug reveal about open source?", ["That open source code cannot be trusted at all", "That critical projects can depend on tiny, underfunded teams", "That encryption is unnecessary", "That only companies should write software"], 1, "OpenSSL protected much of the web but was run by a handful of people on very little money."],
    [5, "What first tipped off Andres Freund to the xz Utils backdoor?", ["A warning from police", "An anonymous email", "A small, unexpected slowdown when logging in", "A virus scanner alert"], 2, "Logins were using more processor time than they should, adding about half a second, and he investigated."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });

WB.L("halting-problem", "technology", "advanced", "Turing and the problems no computer can solve", "Before electronic computers existed, a 23-year-old mathematician proved there are questions no computer, however powerful, will ever be able to answer.",
  [
    ["Hilbert's dream", "In 1928 David Hilbert asked whether a mechanical method could decide whether any mathematical statement was provable.",
      "In the early twentieth century, the German mathematician David Hilbert hoped mathematics could be placed on perfectly solid foundations. In 1928 he and Wilhelm Ackermann posed a challenge known by its German name, the Entscheidungsproblem, or 'decision problem'. Could there be a definite step-by-step procedure that, given any statement in formal logic, would decide whether it could be proved? If so, mathematics might in principle be mechanised, its questions settled by following rules rather than by flashes of insight. Then, in 1931, Kurt Gödel showed that any consistent system rich enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove. But the decision problem was a separate question, and answering it required something nobody yet had: a precise definition of what a 'mechanical procedure' actually is."],
    ["A machine made of thought", "Turing defined computation by imagining a simple machine that reads and writes symbols on an endless tape.",
      "In 1936 Alan Turing, a 23-year-old fellow of King's College, Cambridge, tackled the problem in a paper titled 'On Computable Numbers'. He began by asking what a human clerk does when calculating with pencil and paper, and stripped it down to its bare essentials. The result was an imaginary device now called a Turing machine: an endless tape divided into squares, a head that can read, write or erase one symbol at a time and move left or right, and a finite table of rules saying what to do next, depending on the symbol it sees and its current state. It sounds absurdly simple. Yet Turing argued convincingly that anything that can be calculated by following a precise procedure can be calculated by a machine like this."],
    ["One machine to imitate them all", "Turing showed a single universal machine could carry out any other machine's rules, the idea behind every modern computer.",
      "Turing's paper contained a second startling idea. The rule table of any Turing machine could itself be written down as symbols on a tape. So he described a universal machine which, given a description of any other machine plus its input, would simulate it step by step and produce the same result. In other words, one machine could do the work of every machine, provided it was handed the right instructions. That is exactly what a modern computer is. Your laptop does not need different hardware to be a word processor, a game or a calculator; it simply loads a different program. Historians debate how directly Turing's abstract idea influenced the engineers who built the first stored-program computers in the late 1940s, but the concept was there in 1936."],
    ["The program that never stops", "The halting problem asks whether a given program will eventually finish or run forever.",
      "Anyone who has watched a frozen app knows that programs sometimes get stuck in endless loops. It would be extremely useful to have a perfect checker: a program that could examine any other program and its input and report, in advance, whether it will eventually stop or run forever. For some programs the answer is obvious. A program that counts from one to ten halts; one that says 'repeat forever' does not. Others are fiendishly hard. Consider a program that searches for an even number greater than 2 that is not the sum of two primes, stopping only if it finds one. Whether it ever halts depends on Goldbach's conjecture, an unsolved problem in mathematics since 1742. A perfect halting checker would settle such questions instantly."],
    ["A proof by contradiction", "Turing proved that no general halting checker can exist, using a self-referential trick.",
      "Suppose someone claims to have built a perfect checker; call it H. The argument, in the form it is usually taught today, goes like this. Build a new program, call it Contrary, that takes any program, asks H whether that program halts when given its own code as input, and then does the opposite. If H says it halts, Contrary loops forever; if H says it loops, Contrary stops. Now feed Contrary its own code. If H predicts Contrary halts, Contrary loops; if H predicts it loops, Contrary halts. Either way H is wrong, so no such H can exist. Turing used reasoning of this kind to answer Hilbert: no general mechanical procedure can decide every mathematical question. The American logician Alonzo Church reached the same conclusion independently, by a different route, shortly before."],
    ["Living with the impossible", "Undecidability has practical consequences, such as why no virus scanner or bug checker can ever be perfect.",
      "The halting problem is not a quirk of old mathematics; its consequences run right through computing. A later result, Rice's theorem, extends it: no program can perfectly decide, for every possible program, any meaningful property of how that program behaves, such as whether it will ever leak a password. That is why no antivirus software can be guaranteed to catch every possible piece of malicious code, and why tools that check programs for bugs must accept limits. They can miss problems, raise false alarms or give up on some cases. In practice, engineers work around this with time limits, careful restrictions and checkers that are right most of the time. Turing's 1936 insight remains a humbling boundary. Some questions are not merely hard for computers. They are impossible."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did Hilbert's Entscheidungsproblem ask?", ["Whether computers could think", "Whether a mechanical procedure could decide if any logical statement is provable", "Whether every equation has a solution", "Whether infinity exists"], 1, "Hilbert and Ackermann asked for a step-by-step method that would settle provability for any statement."],
    [1, "Which of these is part of a Turing machine?", ["A keyboard and screen", "A network connection", "An endless tape, a read-write head and a finite table of rules", "Thousands of vacuum tubes"], 2, "Turing's imaginary machine was deliberately minimal, modelled on a clerk working with pencil and paper."],
    [2, "Why is Turing's universal machine so important?", ["It was the first machine to be built from transistors", "It showed one machine could do any other machine's work if given the right instructions", "It broke the Enigma code", "It proved Goldbach's conjecture"], 1, "That is the principle of every modern computer: one piece of hardware, endlessly many programs."],
    [4, "What does the halting proof show?", ["That every program eventually stops", "That no general program can correctly predict, for every program and input, whether it will halt", "That computers are slower than humans", "That loops should never be used"], 1, "Feeding the contrary program its own code forces any claimed perfect checker into a contradiction."],
    [5, "Which practical consequence follows from undecidability?", ["Computers cannot add large numbers", "No antivirus program can be guaranteed to catch every possible piece of malicious code", "Programs cannot be copied", "Internet speeds are limited"], 1, "Rice's theorem means perfect general analysis of program behaviour is impossible, so real tools accept limits."]
  ],
  { added: "2026-10-26" });
